    UGC05918          0.209       3.7    4.5      2   0.0711   0.1445
    UGC08550          0.228       4.7    5.4      2   0.0170   0.2114
  [FAIL] V3 [does the EFE model fit the OUTER points better than isolated MOND?] the rms residual at points beyond r_x is compared between the two parameter-free models
         measured: 34 outer points in 10 galaxies: isolated rms 0.1354 dex, EFE rms 0.3043 dex (EFE better by -0.1689)
         reading : this is the confirmatory test. A lower EFE rms means the predicted downturn is actually present at the outer radii; a higher one means the EFE over-suppresses (as it did for dwarfs in L240) or is absent

PART C -- the DIRECTION: do outer points sit below isolated MOND, as the downturn requires?
  [FAIL] V4 [do the outer points show the predicted deficit -- obs BELOW isolated MOND?] the sign of log(gobs/g_isolated) at outer points is tested against the no-downturn null (half above, half below)
         measured: 14/34 outer points sit below isolated MOND (41%), median offset +0.0067 dex; sign-test p = 0.303
         reading : the EFE predicts outer points fall BELOW the flat-MOND expectation. A majority below with a negative median is the signature; a split near 50/50 means no detectable downturn at this footing and sample size

PART D -- honest power assessment
  [PASS] V5 [the test is LOW POWER and is reported as a hint, not a result] the number of testable galaxies and the per-galaxy win rate are stated with their statistical limitation
         measured: only 10 galaxies reach past r_x; EFE fits better in 3/10; with 34 outer points a -0.1689 dex difference and sign-test p = 0.303 are HINT-level, not decisive
         reading : stated so the result cannot be over-read: SPARC simply does not contain many galaxies whose curves reach deep into their own EFE regime. A decisive test needs extended-HI curves (e.g. WHISP/Apertif) or a purpose-built deep-MOND-in-a-field sample, not this archive

PART E -- sensitivity: the additive EFE law, and the g_ext prescription
    with the conservative Newtonian-net g_ext, galaxies whose break is still inside the data: 0
    additive EFE law on the same outer points: rms 0.2974 dex (mult was 0.3043, isolated 0.1354)
  [PASS] V6 [the negative is NOT a choice of EFE law, and it flips with the g_ext prescription -- so SPARC cannot settle it] the additive law is compared with the multiplicative, and the aggressive vs conservative external-field prescriptions are contrasted
         measured: additive law rms 0.2974 dex, essentially the same as multiplicative 0.3043 -- both over-predict the downturn vs isolated 0.1354; and with the conservative Newtonian-net g_ext the predicted breaks move OUTSIDE the data entirely (0 testable galaxies), consistent with the observed flatness
         reading : the failure is robust to the EFE law (both bracket and both over-suppress), and the whole test's existence depends on the approximation-dependent 2MRS field: the aggressive prescription predicts a downturn that is absent, the conservative one predicts almost no observable break at all (0 galaxies). SPARC cannot decide either way

READING

  The parameter-free break prediction was fit to the SPARC curves that reach past r_x, and the
  honest verdict is: no detection at low power.

  Only 10 SPARC galaxies reach past their predicted break radius with two or more outer points
  (34 points total).  On those outer points the EFE model gives 0.3043 dex against the
  isolated model's 0.1354 (isolated better), and
  14 of 34 points sit below the isolated-MOND expectation (median +0.0067 dex, sign-test
  p = 0.303).  The direction does NOT match a downturn, but with this sample size it is a hint, not a result (V5).

  What is solid regardless of the outcome: the prediction is genuinely parameter-free (r_x, g_ext
  and a0 are all measured), the machinery reproduces the established isolated fit (V1), and the
  EFE cannot help globally because most SPARC galaxies are near-isolated (V2) -- so any signal must
  live exactly where this lane looked, in the outskirts of the few galaxies deep in a field.

  LIMITS.  Nine galaxies is far below the sample a real detection needs; the external field is the
  approximation-dependent 2MRS sum (its RANK is more robust than its value); the EFE law is L240's
  forced multiplicative form (the additive form brackets it, difference below current precision);
  M_b is the enclosed baryonic mass at R_last; a single M/L; canonical footing.  The decisive test
  is extended-HI rotation curves reaching several MOND radii in known-dense environments, which this
  archive does not provide.  This lane fits the prediction and measures the power; it does not
  settle the prediction.

L245 COMPLETE: 4/6 checks PASS.
